=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — total loss of connectivity between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and
pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) due to a failed switch-to-switch cable — does not correspond to
any fault present in the network at this time.

Direct probing shows that end-to-end ICMP traffic flows in both directions with 0%
packet loss and sub-millisecond RTT (~1.1 ms). Every physical interface across all
five routers (leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1,
super_spine_router_0) reports state UP/LOWER_UP — none of the six inter-switch links
(leaf-to-spine and spine-to-super-spine) are down or degraded. All BGP sessions in
the Clos fabric are in the Established state with the expected prefix counts: each
leaf router sees one remote prefix from its two spine peers; each spine router sees
one prefix from each attached leaf and zero from the super-spine (which does not
originate host routes); the super-spine reaches both host subnets via its spine
uplinks. The FIB on every router contains correct best-path entries for both
10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24.

Root cause: there is no fault. The network is fully healthy and the user's premise
(a dead inter-rack cable) is incorrect.

Corrective action: none required on the network side. Advise the user to retest
connectivity from their endpoint; the reported outage may have been transient, caused
by a brief BGP convergence event at lab startup, or simply a misidentified symptom
on the client host rather than the fabric.
=== END ===
